From d2d0fe778dc985f39d4eab0b11e702579cd150e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 16:59:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: rkvenc: Add a CBR frame rate control for
 H.264

H.264 CBR used the generic mode of the H.264 encode core, which does not
work with the VEPU580 row rate control:

- its QP only moves when a picture misses its target by more than 25%,
  and the row rate control keeps every picture within that, so the frame
  QP stays where it started and the row rate control sits at its limit;
- its start QP comes from a fixed table and a P target of 0.8 times the
  nominal size: 1080p at 4 Mb/s starts at QP 41 and needs over 100
  pictures to get near 26;
- the P targets settle at about 0.89 times the nominal size, so the
  bitrate stays 5-9% below the target;
- I and P pictures keep separate QP histories: an IDR uses the I QP of
  the previous GOP and the next P picture, coded against it, overshoots
  its budget about 3.5 times.

Add a frame rate control for the VEPU580 that behaves like the one of the
Rockchip MPP library: one QP state for the stream (P pictures), I
pictures 2 QP below it, a start QP from the bits per pixel of the first I
picture, size targets from a GOP plan with the I/P size ratio and the
IDR period measured on the stream, and the deviation from that plan
spread over the next pictures. The QP moves by 1/8 of the QP error of a
picture (6 * log2(size / target)), and by half of the part beyond three
QP steps.

Board results (RK3588S, 1080p60 and 2160p30 clips of 120-300 pictures,
2-40 Mb/s, GStreamer v4l2slh264enc): the bitrate error goes from -2.8..-9.4%
to -0.6..+2.5%, and PSNR-Y improves by 0.7-3.6 dB at 1080p with the row
rate control left as it is.

Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
